## Passing Ownership to and from Functions

When you pass a value to a function (without `&`), ownership moves into the function. The function can then use, modify, or destroy it.

### Move Into Function

```xiom
fn consume(v: Vec[Int]) -> Unit {
  io.println("Vector length: " + v.len());
  // v goes out of scope — Vec is freed
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let data = Vec[Int]();
  data.push(1);
  consume(data);  // data MOVED into consume
  // data is no longer valid here
  return 0;
}
```

### Move Out of Function (Return)

```xiom
fn create(count: Int) -> Vec[Int] {
  let v = Vec[Int]();
  var i = 0;
  while i < count {
    v.push(i);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return v;  // ownership moves to caller
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let numbers = create(5);  // numbers now owns the Vec
  return numbers.len();
}
```

### Move Through Chain

```xiom
fn step1() -> Vec[Int] {
  let v = Vec[Int]();
  v.push(1);
  return v;
}

fn step2(v: Vec[Int]) -> Vec[Int] {
  v.push(2);  // v is mutable in this scope
  return v;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let v = step1();      // ownership from step1
  let v = step2(v);     // passes ownership, gets it back
  return v.len();       // 2
}
```

### Ownership Pattern: Builder

```xiom
pub type Builder = { items: Vec[Str]; }

fn Builder.new() -> Builder {
  return Builder{ items: Vec[Str]() };
}

fn Builder.add(self: Builder, item: Str) -> Builder {
  self.items.push(item);
  return self;  // return ownership for chaining
}

fn Builder.build(self: Builder) -> Vec[Str] {
  return self.items;
}
```

